# --- 7 intro ---
rep("Table 4 collects the main exercises, all in the fiscal specification, and Figure 4 at the end of the section summarises every variant; the trend-specification counterparts are in Online Appendix Table B3 and change no conclusion. The two placebo tests carry the greatest evidential weight because they are tests the fiscal hypothesis could have failed.",
    "Table 4 collects the main exercises, all in the fiscal specification, and Figure 4 at the end of the section summarises every variant; the trend-specification counterparts are in Online Appendix Table B3 and change no conclusion. Exercises that need data the pre-1975 vintages or surveys do not supply (zero-net-migration projections, publication dating, the EU margin, horizons below ten years, the LTIM basis) are run on the longest sample the data allow and say so. The placebo tests carry the greatest evidential weight because they are tests the fiscal hypothesis could have failed.\n\n*The extension itself.* An earlier version of this paper estimated the specification on 1977–2019, the earliest start the non-EU series allows. Extending the dependent variable back to 1965 on the non-British basis (Section 4.1) adds fourteen years under a different entry regime, a different measurement basis, a different unemployment source, and projections built from five-year age groups with interpolated tenth years, and the projection coefficient moves from 0.0082 to 0.0081. The years added are consistent with the estimate rather than an independent test of it: over 1965–78 the projected change declines almost linearly (its correlation with time is −0.97), so on those years alone a trend would absorb it; on the full sample a linear trend, or step indicators for the Commonwealth Immigrants Act 1968 and the entry into force of the Immigration Act 1971, leave the projection at 0.0084 and 0.0082, because the identifying variation lies from 1979 onwards, where the projection is not trending (correlation with time −0.09). Defining the controlled margin as non-British citizens throughout gives 0.0054, diluted after 2004 by EU inflows the government could not ration; ending that series in 2003, before the A8 accession, restores 0.0106.")
