rep("A HAC Wald test of dropping the projection term gives F(1, 37) = 32.23 (p < 0.001), and the information criterion improves by 14.9 points when it is restored — a difference conventionally taken to leave no support for the restricted model. The functional-form evidence agrees: without the projection, RESET falls to p = 0.12, against p = 0.41 with it, and in the trend specification it rejects outright (p = 0.048 against 0.078).",
    "A HAC Wald test of dropping the projection term gives F(1, 48) = 66.4 (p < 0.001), and the information criterion improves by 23.6 points when it is restored — a difference conventionally taken to leave no support for the restricted model. The functional-form evidence agrees: without the projection, RESET falls to p = 0.055, against p = 0.37 with it.")
rep("Substituting the projected ten-year change in the total dependency ratio for the OADR change eliminates the result: the coefficient falls to 0.0020 (p = 0.16) and is statistically indistinguishable from zero, while the same substitution in every other log-level variant, at every horizon and in levels as in deltas, likewise yields nothing. Measured against the no-projection baseline above, the contrast is sharper than significance alone conveys: the old-age ratio improves the information criterion by 14.9 points, the total ratio by 0.1 (HAC Wald F(1, 37) = 2.11, p = 0.16). The total ratio does not merely fail to reach significance — it barely earns the degree of freedom it costs, where the old-age ratio transforms the fit. A generic demographic confounder, or a data-mined artefact of trending series, has no reason to select the pensioner-specific ratio so cleanly; a fiscal mechanism must.",
    "The projected ten-year change in the total dependency ratio is rebuilt from the same vintages, with the same interpolation before 1975. On 1977–2019 substituting it for the OADR change eliminates the result: the coefficient is 0.0020 (p = 0.16), the same substitution at every horizon and in levels as in deltas likewise yields nothing, and against the no-projection baseline the old-age ratio improves the information criterion by 14.9 points where the total ratio improves it by 0.1. On the extended sample the total ratio is not null on its own (0.0015, p = 0.001): in the 1960s the projected changes in the child and pensioner populations moved together, so the total ratio is then a noisy proxy for the old-age ratio, and it swings between +106 and −64 points across the decade. The horse race settles it. Entered together, the old-age ratio holds at 0.0076 (p < 0.001) and the total ratio falls to 0.0004 (p = 0.48); the total ratio's own equation is also the only one in the battery to fail the Breusch–Godfrey test. A generic demographic confounder, or a data-mined artefact of trending series, has no reason to select the pensioner-specific ratio so cleanly; a fiscal mechanism must.")
