rep("Adding their full indicator set (POL97, POL02 for the 2002 skilled-worker route, and enlargement indicators for 1986, 1995 and 2004) to the fiscal specification leaves the indicators jointly irrelevant under HAC inference (F(5, 32) = 1.09, p = 0.38), while the projection holds at 0.0080 (p = 0.001). The reason is that the indicator nearly discretises the projection: the ten-year delta averages 0.6 points before 1998, 14.9 within the window and −4.5 after 2007, so the literature's dummies were standing in for the projections.",
    "Adding their full indicator set (POL97, POL02 for the 2002 skilled-worker route, and enlargement indicators for 1986, 1995 and 2004) to the fiscal specification leaves the indicators jointly irrelevant under HAC inference (F(5, 43) = 1.09, p = 0.38), while the projection holds at 0.0074 (p < 0.001); the 1998 step alone gives 0.0070 and the 1998–2007 window 0.0072. The reason is that the indicator nearly discretises the projection over the years the literature studies: the ten-year delta averages 14.9 points within the window and −4.5 after 2007, against 0.6 over 1977–97, so the literature's dummies were standing in for the projections.")
rep("Two checks confirm the reading. Interacting the step with the projection returns a negative interaction (−0.0072, p = 0.34), the opposite of what a post-1997 account requires; and in Mitchell, Pain and Riley's own differenced form none of the indicators comes near significance (the smallest p-value is 0.40), while the projection keeps its sign, if not its precision (0.0050, p = 0.18 for non-EU inflows; 0.0057, p = 0.098 for total inflows). Where the indicators do earn their place is on total inflows including the EU margin the government could not ration (F(5, 32) = 5.50, p < 0.001), with the projection falling to 0.0028 (p = 0.31) — the EU placebo arriving from the other direction, reported with the caveat that this specification reaches a maximum VIF of 56 (Online Appendix B).",
    "Two checks on the 1977–2019 sample confirm the reading. Interacting the step with the projection returns a negative interaction (−0.0072, p = 0.34), the opposite of what a post-1997 account requires; and in Mitchell, Pain and Riley's own differenced form none of the indicators comes near significance (the smallest p-value is 0.40), while the projection keeps its sign, if not its precision (0.0050, p = 0.18 for non-EU inflows; 0.0057, p = 0.098 for total inflows). Where the indicators do earn their place is on total inflows including the EU margin the government could not ration (F(5, 32) = 5.50, p < 0.001), with the projection falling to 0.0028 (p = 0.31) — the EU placebo arriving from the other direction, reported with the caveat that this specification reaches a maximum VIF of 56 (Online Appendix B).")
rep("On gross non-EU inflows, with no population estimate on either side of the equation, the projection coefficient is 0.0082 (HAC s.e. 0.0015), identical to its scaled counterpart, significant beyond the 0.1 per cent level, with clean diagnostics. In levels rather than logs the pattern holds and acquires a directly interpretable magnitude: each further point of projected ten-year OADR deterioration is associated with roughly 1,300 to 1,400 additional gross arrivals per year (1,379, HAC s.e. 318). The choice of tax measure can be tested rather than argued (Online Appendix Tables B4 and B5). Replacing national accounts taxes per working-age person with the same taxes per resident, or with income tax and National Insurance contributions — with or without VAT — on either denominator, leaves the projection coefficient between 0.0081 and 0.0098 and the tax term significant at 1 per cent in every case; measuring the burden as a share of GDP instead removes the tax term (p = 0.70 for all taxes, p = 0.53 for labour taxes, p = 0.43 with VAT added) and weakens the specification (RESET p = 0.026 and 0.007), a share of output being a burden on no one in particular.",
    "On gross controlled inflows, with no population estimate on either side of the equation, the projection coefficient is 0.0085 (HAC s.e. 0.0010), significant beyond the 0.1 per cent level. In levels rather than logs the pattern holds and acquires a directly interpretable magnitude: each further point of projected ten-year OADR deterioration is associated with roughly 1,300 additional gross arrivals per year (1,335, HAC s.e. 255). The choice of tax measure can be tested rather than argued (Online Appendix Tables B4 and B5). Replacing national accounts taxes per working-age person with the same taxes per resident (0.0077) or with income tax and National Insurance contributions per working-age person (0.0091) leaves the projection where it was and the tax term significant at 1 per cent; the household-tax and VAT variants, available from 1975, give 0.0081 to 0.0098 on 1977–2019. Measuring the burden as a share of GDP instead removes the tax term (p = 0.66) and weakens the specification (RESET p = 0.047), a share of output being a burden on no one in particular.")
rep("Measurement basis. On the LTIM series (1991 onward), which corrects the IPS's known deficiencies with Home Office and Labour Force Survey data, the projection coefficient is larger and significant at the 0.1 per cent level (0.0159, HAC s.e. 0.0040, on 28 observations),",
    "*Measurement basis.* On the LTIM series (1991 onward), which corrects the IPS's known deficiencies with Home Office and Labour Force Survey data, the projection coefficient is larger and significant at the 0.1 per cent level (0.0159, HAC s.e. 0.0040, on 28 observations),")
rep("The projected level of the OADR at the ten-year horizon, in place of the projected change, returns an almost identical coefficient (0.0085 against 0.0082, p < 0.001), and for a reason that supports the change formulation rather than competing with it: the level is the current ratio plus the projected change, so the level model constrains the two to a common coefficient. Entered separately, the projection holds at 0.0083 while the current ratio contributes nothing (0.0002, p = 0.95), and the equality restriction is rejected (F(1, 36) = 8.28, p = 0.007), which is why the level form fits worse (AIC −63.9 against −70.1). The change formulation is retained as the measure of news relative to the inherited position, and the data agree. Figure 3 plots the coefficient at every horizon from one to ten years.",
    "The projected level of the OADR at the ten-year horizon, in place of the projected change, returns a similar coefficient (0.0075 against 0.0081, p = 0.001), and for a reason that supports the change formulation rather than competing with it: the level is the current ratio plus the projected change, so the level model constrains the two to a common coefficient. Entered separately, the projection holds at 0.0084 while the current ratio contributes nothing (0.0004, p = 0.83), and the equality restriction is rejected (F(1, 47) = 41.2, p < 0.001), which is why the level form fits worse by 15.6 points of the information criterion. The change formulation is retained as the measure of news relative to the inherited position, and the data agree. Figure 3 plots the coefficient at every horizon from one to ten years on the 1977–2019 years for which the vintages publish every horizon.")
rep("*Dynamics.* The fiscal specification's residuals show serial correlation at the third and fourth orders (Section 6.2). A second lag of the dependent variable does not absorb it: the added lag is small and insignificant (−0.06, p = 0.65), the information criterion worsens, the Breusch–Godfrey tests do not improve, and the projection coefficient is unchanged at 0.0080.",
    "*Dynamics.* The fiscal specification's residuals show serial correlation at the second to fourth orders (Section 6.2). A second lag of the dependent variable does not absorb it: the added lag is small and insignificant (−0.05, p = 0.65), the information criterion worsens by 4.8 points, the Breusch–Godfrey tests do not improve, and the projection coefficient is unchanged at 0.0080.")
rep("*Timing placebo.* With 43 annual observations, a finite-sample check that does not lean on asymptotic inference is worth having. Rotating the projection series through the sample years keeps its own persistence and distribution but breaks its timing relative to inflows; re-estimating under each of the 42 rotations gives a distribution of placebo t-statistics against which the actual one can be ranked. None comes close: the largest absolute t-statistic among the rotations is 3.97, against 5.68 for the actual alignment, so the randomisation p-value is 1/43 = 0.023, the smallest this design can deliver.",
    "*Timing placebo.* With 55 annual observations, a finite-sample check that does not lean on asymptotic inference is worth having. Rotating the projection series through the sample years keeps its own persistence and distribution but breaks its timing relative to inflows; re-estimating under each of the 54 rotations gives a distribution of placebo t-statistics against which the actual one can be ranked. None comes close: the largest absolute t-statistic among the rotations is 5.33, against 8.15 for the actual alignment, so the randomisation p-value is 1/55 = 0.018, the smallest this design can deliver.")
rep("*Sample and inference.* Results survive beginning the sample in 1979, 1983 or 1987, the substitution of the trend for the tax burden, and OLS, HAC or Newey–West inference, so the result is not carried by any single political era, entry regime, or macroeconomic episode. Figure 4 collects the projection coefficient from every variant in this section: all fifteen intervals exclude zero and overlap the baseline, while both placebo intervals include zero.",
    "*Sample and inference.* Results survive beginning the sample in 1971, 1977 or 1979 (0.0079, 0.0082, 0.0081), ending it in 2003, the substitution of the trend for the tax burden, and OLS, HAC or Newey–West inference, so the result is not carried by any single political era, entry regime, or macroeconomic episode. Figure 4 collects the projection coefficient from every variant in this section: all twenty-one intervals exclude zero and overlap the baseline, while the EU placebo and the total-dependency term estimated alongside the old-age ratio include it; the total-dependency term on its own does not, for the reason given above.\n\n*Stability over time.* Re-estimating the fiscal specification on expanding samples gives 0.0088 with the sample ending in 1990, 0.0083 in 1994, 0.0101 in 1998, 0.0110 in 2001 and 0.0082 in 2010, every one within the full-sample band. Rolling 25-year windows return coefficients between −0.001 and 0.013, significant at 5 per cent in 23 of the 31 windows; the exceptions are the windows that lie entirely within 1966–1995, in which the projections barely moved. The OLS-CUSUM test does not reject constancy (p = 0.99), and on 1979–2019 the sup-F test for a single break at an unknown date returns p = 0.46. The relationship is not a creature of any sub-period, although its precision depends on the variation in projections that began in the mid-1990s.\n\n*The instrument.* The projection should move the lever governments actually held, not only the outcome. Work permits and first permissions, the main control on labour migration from outside the Commonwealth and, after 1973, from outside the EEC, are available annually from 1946 to 2008 (Bauer and Salt, 2020); the series is read from their Figure 1 and checked against the totals in their Annex 1. Because permits were set as numbers rather than growth rates, gross permits in levels are the reported form, with the same right-hand side as the fiscal specification. On 1965–2008 the projection enters at 0.62 thousand permits (p < 0.001), about 620 additional permits a year for each point of projected ten-year OADR rise, and at 0.54 thousand (p = 0.010) on 1979–2008; entered alongside, the total dependency ratio contributes nothing (0.035, p = 0.35) while the old-age ratio holds at 0.55 (p = 0.003). Adding permits to the inflow equation leaves the projection coefficient unchanged, so the response to projected ageing ran through work permits and, in larger part, through the other routes the government controlled.\n\n*Realised spending.* The Treasury could be responding to bills already arriving rather than to those projected. The historical public finances database divides spending by function, with social security split between pensioners and non-pensioners from 1978–79 (OBR, 2026). On 1979–2019, adding the previous year's spending on health, on pensioners, on non-pensioners, or on health and pensions together — as shares of GDP, and health also per working-age person — leaves the projection between 0.0074 and 0.0089, significant at 1 per cent throughout, while the spending term is never significant (p ≥ 0.65) and never improves the information criterion. Replacing the projection with realised spending worsens the criterion by 9 to 18 points. Controlled migration tracks projected costs, not incurred ones.\n\n*Supply-side controls.* The projection could be standing in for a determinant of migration the model omits. Four that the literature identifies and that can be measured across the whole sample enter lagged (Hatton and Williamson, 2002; Mitchell and Pain, 2003; Hatton, 2005; Mitchell, Pain and Riley, 2011): the income gap between the UK and seven large non-EU origins — India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, China, Nigeria, the Philippines and South Africa — and, separately, the United States and Australia (Bolt and van Zanden, 2024); the share of those seven populations aged 15–24 (United Nations, 2024); and networks, measured as controlled inflows over the three preceding years per thousand working-age persons. These are determinants of who wishes to come, not of policy, and their role here is the omitted-variable check. Added one at a time the projection is 0.0078, 0.0080 and 0.0084, and 0.0094 with all three; the controls themselves are null apart from the origin youth share (p = 0.06). Real growth, inflation and British emigration per working-age person, the emigration regime of the 1960s and 1970s, leave the projection at 0.0067 (p < 0.001), with none of the three significant. On 1979–2019 the projection's revisions are not predictable from the supply-side variables (F(4, 16) = 1.96, p = 0.15). Under a controlled regime, supply conditions should have little purchase on how many are admitted, and they do not.\n\n*British citizens.* A second uncontrolled flow gives a less tidy answer than the EU margin. British citizens arriving from outside the EU (identified from 1975; ONS, 2020a) are not subject to entry control, yet the projection enters their inflow equation at 0.0065 (p = 0.04) on 1977–2019. The result is weak where the controlled result is strong: on 1979–2019 adding the projection improves the fit of the British equation by 2.8 points of the information criterion against 15.6 for non-EU inflows, the effect disappears in either half of the sample, and half the year-to-year variation in the British series is sampling error (published intervals of ±17 per cent, against ±9 per cent for non-EU inflows). It is also not where returning emigrants would put it: British citizens returning from Oceania and North America show no response, while the effect is concentrated in arrivals from Sub-Saharan Africa (0.0178, p = 0.012) and Asia (0.0074, p = 0.024), regions where, as former parts of the Empire, many British passport holders are citizens by descent or registration rather than emigrants who left the UK (Sriskandarajah and Drew, 2006), a population that nationality law shaped deliberately through the British Nationality Act 1981 (Tyler, 2010), the British Nationality (Hong Kong) Act 1990 and the Nationality, Immigration and Asylum Act 2002. Part of this flow is therefore likely to be first-time migration that resembles non-EU immigration in every respect but the passport, and whose size was itself a matter of policy. Controlling for the British flow directly leaves the projection at 0.0061 (p < 0.001), a lower bound on the policy response.")
